        "commit_id": "5cd09752b24b65aac1088dacb28133f7186861ec",
        "description": "docs: amend \u00a712 rule 3 \u2014 writing is tiered, and the lane is the review step\n\nCHARTER AMENDMENT, decided by the charter owner on 2026-09-05.\n\nWHAT THE RULE WAS. \"Anything that writes tracked source without a person\npresent. Dependency updates, code generation, autofix beyond formatting. The\nwrite is a change nobody reviewed, and the plane has no review step.\"\n\nWHY IT WENT. Its stated reason had expired. \"The plane has no review step\" was\ntrue when written; gitman is now in 26 of the 54 registered repositories and is\nexactly that step \u2014 a lane is a named review queue, it cannot reach trunk except\nthrough `land`, and `gitman undo` reverts a whole intent through jj's op-log.\n011 \u00a78.2 named review, output ownership and audit as the machinery an amendment\nwould need, and left the decision to the charter owner: \"no measurement in this\ndocument makes it.\"\n\nAND 015 MEASURED WHAT THE RULE COST. It is the binding constraint on this\nplane's content: 83 % of every run the plane has ever made is one repository's\nformatter and one nightly janitor, there is one trigger, and rule 3 killed more\n015 candidates than any other rule. A rule that forbids dependency updates, code\ngeneration and autofix forbids most of what an agentic development plane is for.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOW. Three tiers, by where the write lands.\n  A \u2014 free: a file that did not exist, and agent surface (.agents/**, docs/**,\n      notes, a tool's hidden directory).\n  B \u2014 on a lane: every edit to an existing tracked source file. A gitman lane or\n      branch, for a person to merge. Writing the working tree directly instead\n      is breaking this rule, not complying with it.\n  C \u2014 refused: an unattended write to trunk. That invariant replaces the rule.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE. Rule 2 stands and the lane stays local \u2014 no publish, no push,\nno land; creating a lane is reversible on this machine, pushing it is not. Rule\n4 stands and is the likeliest way a generator dies. \u00a78's watcher argument stands\nand was never rule 3's job: `.devman/.runs/` is watcher-ignored, A LANE IS NOT,\nso a tier-B workflow writing `**/*.py` under `format` will fire `format`.\n\nWHAT IS OWED AND NOT BUILT. The amendment is deliberately weaker than a flat\nrefusal, so it needs three things that do not exist: output ownership (a\nworkflow states its paths and tier), a doctor audit for a writer that states\nnone, and lane hygiene (a scheduled tier-B workflow makes 54 lanes a night).\nNo tier-B workflow should ship before the first two.\n\nALSO: the 015 disk-allocation guard is WITHDRAWN. It was chosen by frequency in\nthe human's shell history, which is the wrong signal for a plane that serves\nagentic workflows, and it was machine-scope in a project about 54 repositories.\nRESULT.md \u00a75.1 keeps the error on the record.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
