        "description": "feat: output ownership, and doctor audits it \u2014 the debt \u00a712 rule 3's amendment owed\n\n015 amended \u00a712 rule 3 from a flat refusal into three tiers and recorded that\nthe amendment owed three things it did not have. This pays the first two: a\nworkflow can now declare what it writes, and `doctor` checks the declaration.\n\nWHY A DECLARATION IS NEEDED AT ALL. A flat refusal needs no audit \u2014 nothing was\npermitted, so nothing could be wrong. A tier is a CLAIM, and an unchecked claim\nis weaker than the refusal it replaced.\n\nWHERE IT LIVES, AND WHY NOT IN THE WORKFLOW. The same measurement that put the\ntrigger map in its own file (A5), re-verified here:\n  - a top-level `writes:` key fails `dagu validate` outright;\n  - `tags:` IS accepted and is useless \u2014 a label map charset of `a-zA-Z0-9-_.`,\n    so `devman:writes=lane` dies on the `:`, and no glob with `/` or `*` can be\n    written at all.\nBoth alternative homes are closed, so this takes the third: `writes.toml`, group\nlayer resolved in Nix, repository layer resolved in Python, into the registry\nentry \u2014 exactly the shape `triggers.toml` already has.\n\nIT MERGES PER WORKFLOW, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE IT DIFFERS from \u00a77.3 and from\n`triggers`. Those replace whole-file because a partial result is unpredictable\nfrom either file alone. Here the unit is already one workflow, so a later group\ndeclaring [regen] must not silently drop an earlier group's [format] \u2014 a dropped\ndeclaration is worse than a surprising one, because the audit then reports\nnothing.\n\nA THIRD TIER, `insitu`, BECAUSE THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE AMENDMENT LOST FORMAT'S\nEXCEPTION. Writing `groups/format/writes.toml` is what exposed it: with only\n`free` and `lane`, `format` \u2014 the one writer the charter blesses \u2014 became a\nfinding against itself. The rule it replaced ended \"format is the single\nexception and it is bounded three ways: one glob, a content hash, and its own\ngroup\", and `insitu` keeps that sentence enforceable instead of deleting it.\nWhat separates it from `lane` is the KIND of write: an idempotent normalisation\nof a file the trigger already watched. It adds no content and a second run\nchanges nothing. Code generation, dependency updates and autofix are none of\nthose, and they are `lane`.\n\nWHAT `doctor` DECIDES, and it is set membership rather than heuristic, so \u00a715.7\ndoes not reach it: an unknown tier, a declaration naming a workflow the project\ndoes not project, and \u2014 the one that matters \u2014 a `tier = \"free\"` claim over a\npath outside agent surface. A free-tier write lands in the working tree with\nnobody present, so claiming it for `src/**` is claiming to edit code unattended,\nwhich is what the lane exists to prevent.\n\nWHAT IT REFUSES TO PRETEND. A project that declares nothing is reported\n\"unaudited, not proven silent\". `doctor` reads YAML and a TOML table; it does\nnot run the step.\n\nNO SCHEMA BUMP, DELIBERATELY. Schema 2 added `workflows` and 3 added `triggers`,\nand both bumped because a RUN-TIME reader depends on them \u2014 `run.resolve()` and\nthe watcher. `writes` has no run-time reader: the projection writes it and\n`doctor` reads it, same binary. An older doctor ignores the key and loses a\ncheck it never had. Bumping would instead make every projected entry on this\nmachine report \"a newer devman wrote these entries\" until every shell is\nre-entered, for a field nothing at run time reads.\n\nAlso corrected: 015 RESULT.md \u00a70 claimed `doctor` does not exit 1 on `daemon\nshell`. That was measured against the INSTALLED 0.3.0 build, which does not\ncarry the check. From source it does exit 1, exactly as the kickoff said. The\nbinary on PATH is not the code in the tree \u2014 014's lesson in a new place.\n\nStill owed: lane hygiene. A scheduled tier-B workflow makes a lane per run per\nrepository, and 54 lanes a night is rule 7 in a new hat. No tier-B workflow\nshould ship until that is answered.\n\nGates: base:check 0, base:test 0, pytest 414 passed (398 + 16 new).\n`doctor` from source reports only the pre-existing `daemon shell` finding.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
