        "change_id": "zmrznwuymxpyplkrkkyqrmmsurwstnlu",
        "commit_id": "d51d86d4a736ec1aebb198ff1f75854b8ac301b0",
        "description": "feat: doctor sees what local libraries actually resolve to \u2014 and it is not a pin\n\nFOUR INVESTIGATIONS, THREE DEAD CANDIDATES, ONE CHECK. This commit is the\nsurvivor, and the three deaths are the more useful half.\n\nWHAT WAS PROPOSED: a workflow that updates a repository's stale local-library\ninputs and leaves the result on a lane for review \u2014 the first tier-B writer\nunder \u00a712 rule 3 as amended.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT BUILT. `devenv.lock` records a local `git+file:` input as\n`{type, url}` with NO rev and NO narHash. devenv's generated resolver hands that\nattrset straight to `builtins.fetchTree`, which resolves it LIVE. Proven twice:\n`nix eval` on fetchTree returns the source repo's HEAD exactly, and `devenv\nupdate shellij` on a throwaway copy produced a BYTE-IDENTICAL lockfile. 91 of 93\nlocal inputs on this machine are that shape; the other 2 are real flake.lock\npins. An update workflow would therefore have opened 52 empty branches \u2014 \u00a712\nrule 4, success indistinguishable from doing nothing, the rule that killed\nfull-test.\n\nTHE EXPOSURE UNDERNEATH IT IS THE REAL FINDING, and it is the inverse of the\npremise. An unpinned input follows its source's WORKING TREE, not its HEAD. A\nsource with uncommitted changes is consumed as those uncommitted changes:\nfetchTree returns `rev: NONE` and a `-dirty` marker. Measured now: shellij holds\n15 uncommitted files INCLUDING devenv.nix \u2014 the module it exports \u2014 and 51 of\nthe 54 registered repositories consume it. Nothing said so. This check says so.\n\nALSO KILLED, AND WORTH RECORDING. A fan-out that verifies a library's consumers\nwhen its HEAD moves: 51 consumers x ~70 s measured floor = ~66 min of serial\nwork per fire, on ~24 days a month. It has no legal shape either \u2014 \u00a712 rule 9,\n`dag.run` bypasses every queue and `dag.enqueue` never tells the parent the\nchild failed, so a fan-out can respect the queue or learn the result, never\nboth. The cheap version is this check: record the state, verify nothing.\n\nIT IS CHEAP BECAUSE IT READS SOURCES, NOT CONSUMERS. 54 consumers, 8 distinct\nsources: two git calls per SOURCE and none per consumer. Measured 104 ms, best\nof 3, over the live 54-project registry. That arithmetic is what makes it a\ncheck and not a fan-out.\n\nWHAT IT CANNOT SEE, stated in the docstring: it reads lockfiles, not\nevaluations. An input named but never used is counted; a library reached some\nother way is not.\n\nTESTS ARE HERMETIC ON PURPOSE. The first version shelled out to real git and\npassed in the devenv shell, then failed `base:test` \u2014 `nix flake check` runs the\nsuite in a sandbox with no git on PATH. `_source_state` is stubbed instead; the\ncheck's logic is what is under test, not git's behaviour.\n\nALSO: groups/base/README.md stopped teaching a retracted rule. It still stated\n\"one workflow, one step\" as law with criterion 14 holding BY CONSTRUCTION. 013\nretracted that (e1c227f) and release.yaml has had three steps since stage 4. The\nsurviving argument is the arithmetic \u2014 one devenv invocation is 1.44 s and three\nare 4.3 s. Steps are cheap; devenv calls are not.\n\nGates: base:check 0, base:test 0, doctor 0, pytest 422 passed (+5).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
